l-type-specific and defined in the Pathway Knowledge Base.\n\nCell memory. [truncated]","original_chars":4640},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Methods","heading_level":2,"line_start":175,"line_end":278,"text":"### Resource Availability\n\n#### Lead Contact\n\nFurther information and requests for resources should be directed to and will be fulfilled by the lead contact, Lianghua Wang (lhwang@smmu.edu.cn).\n\n#### Materials Availability\n\nThis study did not generate new reagents or biological materials.\n\n#### Data and Code Availability\n\n- All simulation source code, knowledge base YAML files, and analysis scripts are available at https://github.com/dawnmengsjtu/cellswarm.\n- Simulation output data and intermediate results have been deposited at Zenodo (DOI will be provided upon publication).\n- The TNBC single-cell reference dataset is publicly available at GEO: GSE176078 (Wu et al. 2021).\n- No original code beyond that described above was generated for this study.\n\n### Method Details\n\n#### Cell Agent Architecture\n\nEach cell in CELLSWARM is modeled as an autonomous agent with four architectural layers.\n\nPersistent state. Every agent maintains a mutable state vector comprising: cell\\_type (one of CD8 /g3397 T, Tumor, Treg, Macrophage, NK, B cell), two-dimensional grid position, energy (consumed by actions and replenished by glucose uptake), activation level, exhaustion level, proliferation rate, immune evasion score, suppressive activity, and polarization state. These scalar attributes are updated deterministically each simulation step before the decision phase.\n\nPathway state. Each agent carries a 14-dimensional signaling vector where every pathway takes a continuous value in /g46700,1/g4671 . [truncated]","original_chars":12600},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Method Details","heading_level":3,"line_start":194,"line_end":278,"text":"#### Cell Agent Architecture\n\nEach cell in CELLSWARM is modeled as an autonomous agent with four architectural layers.\n\nPersistent state. Every agent maintains a mutable state vector comprising: cell\\_type (one of CD8 /g3397 T, Tumor, Treg, Macrophage, NK, B cell), two-dimensional grid position, energy (consumed by actions and replenished by glucose uptake), activation level, exhaustion level, proliferation rate, immune evasion score, suppressive activity, and polarization state. These scalar attributes are updated deterministically each simulation step before the decision phase.\n\nPathway state. Each agent carries a 14-dimensional signaling vector where every pathway takes a continuous value in /g46700,1/g4671 . The pathways are organized into five functional modules: immune activation (TCR, CD28), immune checkpoints (PD-1, CTLA-4), metabolism (mTOR, AMPK, HIF-1 α ), cytokine signaling (IFNγ /JAK-STAT1, IL-2/JAK-STAT5, TGFβ /SMAD, NFκ B), proliferation and cell death (PI3K/AKT, MAPK/ERK, caspase). Pathway values are computed each step via a sigmoid activation function σ /g4666 x /g4667/g34041//g46661/g3397 e /g3398 /g4667 applied to a weighted sum of environmental signals and internal state variables. For example, in a CD8 /g3397 T cell: TCR/g3404 σ /g46660.5/g1668tumor\\_nearby/g33970.3/g1668activation/g4667 and $\\text{PD-1} = \\sigma(1.2 \\cdot \\text{PD\\text{-}L1\\_local} + 0.5 \\cdot \\text{exhaustion})$. Weights are cell-type-specific and defined in the Pathway Knowledge Base.\n\nCell memory. [truncated]","original_chars":11820}],"docling_markdown":"data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_003902.md","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003902","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The paper presents a new model, CELLSWARM, rather than a review or wrapper. It is centered on tumor microenvironment cell simulation and uses an LLM as the cognitive core of the cell agents.","evidence_snippet":"\"replaces rule-based cell decision-making with large language model (LLM)-driven autonomous agents\"; \"recapitulates TNBC microenvironment composition\"; \"Each cell in CELLSWARM is modeled as an autonomous agent\"; \"LLM serving as its cognitive core\"; \"structured knowledge bases for cancer-specific context\"; \"LLM-driven cell agents\"; \"cell-level agents in tumor microenvironment simulations\"; \"weights are cell-type-specific and defined in the Pathway Knowledge Base\"; \"sensing of indirect genetic perturbations\"; \"cancer-specific context\"; \"direct checkpoint knockouts\"; \"IFNγ knockout experiment\"; \"tumor microenvironment\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003902","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The paper presents an LLM-driven simulation framework rather than a new generative text-bio model. The candidate contribution is a wrapper that uses an LLM as the cognitive core for cell agents and demonstrates downstream simulation behavior.","evidence_snippet":"“replaces rule-based cell decision-making with large language model (LLM)-driven autonomous agents” and “LLM serving as its cognitive core”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}}]

